---
/**
 * How a health claim earns its place.
 *
 * This replaced a horizontal bar chart. Two reasons, one design and one legal.
 *
 * Design: a filled-track bar chart is dashboard furniture. It read as data on
 * a page that has no data, and it made the argument look like a measurement
 * rather than a principle.
 *
 * Legal: bar LENGTH encodes magnitude. Drawing "systematic review" four and a
 * half times longer than "anecdote" states a quantified difference in support
 * that we cannot evidence, which is exactly the line the Medicine
 * (Advertisement and Sale) Act 1956 sits on. Weight and ink carry the same
 * ordering without asserting a quantity, so nothing here can be read as a
 * measurement.
 *
 * It is an ordered list, so it is marked up as one. An <ol> gives screen
 * readers the ranking for free, where the old SVG needed a paragraph of
 * <desc> to explain what the bars meant.
 *
 * Motion is the argument, not decoration: the rungs arrive weakest first and
 * the rule draws downward behind them, so the eye climbs the hierarchy in the
 * order the copy claims it should be read. Under reduced motion the whole
 * thing is simply present, fully legible, no sequence.
 */
const rungs = [
  { k: 'Anecdote', t: 'One person said it worked.' },
  { k: 'Testimonial', t: 'Several people said so.' },
  { k: 'Observational study', t: 'Patterns watched over time.' },
  { k: 'Controlled trial', t: 'Compared against a control group.' },
  { k: 'Systematic review', t: 'Every good trial weighed together.' },
];
---
<figure class="evidence">
  <figcaption class="evidence-lede">
    The useful question is not whether a claim is true. It is how anyone would know.
  </figcaption>

  <p class="evidence-pole">Weaker grounds for belief</p>

  <ol class="evidence-ladder" data-reveal>
    {rungs.map((r, i) => (
      <li class="rung" style={`--i:${i}; --step:${(i + 1) / rungs.length}`}>
        <span class="rung-name">{r.k}</span>
        <span class="rung-gloss">{r.t}</span>
      </li>
    ))}
  </ol>

  <p class="evidence-pole evidence-pole--strong">Stronger grounds for belief</p>

  <p class="evidence-note">
    A general appraisal hierarchy. It describes how evidence is weighed, and makes
    no claim about any treatment.
  </p>
</figure>
